WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.537
Automaticity is practiced execution with reduced conscious effort.

2
00:00:03.537 --> 00:00:12.820
It allows routine components of a task to run smoothly so the performer can reserve attention for judgment, monitoring, and adaptation.

3
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:22.988
A skilled operator, engineer, analyst, writer, or communicator may perform common steps fluently because those steps have been practiced many times with feedback.

4
00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:26.083
Automaticity is powerful because attention is limited.

5
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:31.388
Working memory can only hold and process a small amount at once.

6
00:00:31.388 --> 00:00:38.461
If every basic step consumes full attention, there is little capacity left for higher-level decisions.

7
00:00:38.461 --> 00:00:46.860
When routine components become efficient, the performer can notice exceptions, coordinate with others, and think about the larger situation.

8
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.629
The risk is autopilot.

9
00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:53.050
Fluent behavior can continue even when the situation has changed.

10
00:00:53.050 --> 00:01:04.102
In high-risk work, experts still need checklists, procedures, deliberate pauses, and peer review because memory and attention can fail under pressure, fatigue, or interruption.

11
00:01:04.102 --> 00:01:06.754
These supports do not replace expertise.

12
00:01:06.754 --> 00:01:09.849
They protect expertise from predictable human limits.

13
00:01:09.849 --> 00:01:13.385
Attention control includes focus routines and interruption management.

14
00:01:13.385 --> 00:01:23.553
Before important work, a performer may clarify the goal, remove avoidable distractions, set up required tools, and define when to pause or escalate.

15
00:01:23.553 --> 00:01:30.626
During the work, they may use checkbacks, notes, or short reviews to keep track of assumptions.

16
00:01:30.626 --> 00:01:38.584
The point is to make attention a managed resource rather than hoping it will remain stable by itself.
